Server architecture
===================

Basic elements:

- Tcp Server. Takes care of holding sockets, sending data to them, calling callbacks when receives data or connections are closed.
- Client manager. Lists clients, all the data associated to them.
- Table. Lists game objects.
- Supervisor. Analyses requests from clients, and decide if they are applicable (user clearance, coherence of the request, etc). Then applies changes and takes care of warning back users.

The main loop should be blocked at each frame by the Tcp Server. All server activity should normally be triggered by incoming connections/data.


1. Tcp Server
-------------

Deals by itself with the keeping of client sockets: accepts every incoming connection, adds then to its list, calls a callback. Calls callback for every incoming data, as well as for lost connections. Allows to send data to a particular socket, or to all, or to all but one/two. Simple.

2. Client manager
-----------------

Identifies clients uniquely by their nicknames (for command targets) or sockets (for command senders). So, two tables, one indexed by nicks, one by socket, all point to the same client objects.
Client object:
- a nickname (contains anything but blanks)
- a socket
- a clearance (0:observer, 1:voice, 2:hand, 3:host)
- a color

3. Table
--------

Lists game objects by unique identifiers (numbers). Just a table with helper functions (add, remove, etc). See more details about game objects later. Identifiers are not reused to avoid confusion (the table can be sparse).

4. Supervisor
-------------

The God of the house. It parses incoming commands, rejects the malformed ones and checks appliability and conformity to protocol by checking table status and player data. Then instructs the game status change, then informs users of what each of them needs to know about it through the network.

5. Game entities
----------------

Entity: has a position,angle,scale,second scale (if there is a second then they are for x and y), a visibility flag (turned off when in a container for instance)
	BasicEntity: can be added to collections (pile, stack, bag). Can be compared to another one.
		Label: has a string. Can also be attached to another entity, in which case it follows it (has relative position). Can have a second string. Has a flipping capacity (= hiding if only one string).
		N-Image: has an indefinite number of sprite names  (string "package/filename"). Has a current sprite number (the "up" side). Can be flipped to the next, or to a random one. Has a modulating color.
			[Image: has only one sprite, so doesn't present flipping ability. Unsure.]
			[Card just a 2-image, not necessary]
	Pile: has one BasicE. One number. Can be splited. To add an BasicE to it, it has to be identical (same sprites, same color/same text). The added one is destroyed.
	Stack: has an ordered list of BasicE. They can be pushed/popped. The stack can be splitted. Can be reversed (turned upside down): 2-Images/labels are then flipped.
		Deck: a special stack for cards. Has a shuffle operation. Shows (and tells about) only the top element's [perhaps the number of elements].
	Counter: just has a number there, setable.
		Die: a counter with a random number generator. Randomizable. Range setable.
	Clock: holds a number, the server time at last reset. Can be reset, paused, started. May need synchronisation with clients (sending them the server time every once in a while so they can know the approximate time difference, and adjust "their" server time)
	Bag: has an unordered list of N-Images. Shows none. Can randomly pick a certain number to put them outside.
	
	
6. Protocol: client to server
-----------------------------

Text based, encoded in UTF8. Words are space separated. All commands have the following syntax:

COMMAND [arguments...]

Where arguments is a sequence of space separated strings. Arguments in brackets are optionnal. Arguments in quote mean the strings appear there litteraly (without the quotes).
All strings arguments are escaped with the following conversion, to be sure to have space separated arguments:
	spaces => %s
	newlines => %n
	% => %p


x. Connecting
-------------

Upon connecting, a user gets special clearance -1 (connecting) and has to handshake with:

HANDSHAKE Do you want to play a game?

When done, the user gets clearance level 2 (hand. If there are no hosts yet, user get level 3) and is revealed to the other users. If a user doesn't handshake before a given time, the server can drop him.

Commands NICK and COLOR can be sent before the HANDSHAKE so that the client will appear directly to the others with appropriate settings.

a. User data and chatting
-------------------------

NICK nickname
	Request to change nick to nickname. Fails if nickname is already in use. If it contains a space, only the first word is considered.

COLOR r g b
	Request to change user's color to (r,g,b), where r g and b are decimal numbers in the range 0:255.

SAY message
	Request to send message to the chat channel. Fails if clearance is less than 1 (voice).
	
TELL nickname message
	Request to send private message to user nickname. Fails if clearance is less than 1 (voice). Private messages are signaled to the other users with a message such as "nickname1 whispered to nickname2" (see protocol server -> client).

QUIT
	Properly announce parting. The server can then make all necessary cleaning. If the user was the only host (clearance level 3), a random user with hand (level 2) is promoted to host (or if none, a level 1, and if none, 0). Even if no users are left, the server goes on and keeps the game state.
	
PROMOTE nickname level
	Request promotion of user nickname to clearance level level, where level is a number in the range 0:3. Fails if a level 2 (hand) requests any promotion to host, or demotion from host. For voices and observers, the command always fails.

KICK nickname
	Request deconnection of user nickname. Fails if clearance is inferior than 3 (host).
	
PING
	Request for a PONG (see protocol server -> client).
	
b. Manipulation of game entities
--------------------------------

(All these commands require clearance 2 or 3 to succeed.)
As a general rule, the server should try to be as nice as possible. If an action does not apply or is irrelevant, just ignore it, and if necessary, notify in an answer but never block in error.

MOVE id x y
	Move entity id to position (x,y)
	
ROTATE id a
	Rotate entity id to angle a.
	
SCALE id s1 [s2]
	If only s1 is present, entity id's scale to s1. Else, set its scale on x to s1, and on y to s2.

FLIP id ["random"]
	Flip current entity to its next state (next sprite for an N-Image, second face for a label, reverse a stack). If "random" is present, flip to a random side of an N-Image. 

DELETE id
	Remove entity id from the table.
	
MOVEZ id pos
	Change entity's id to pos by inserting it there (others shall move up until they find an unused id), as described by Table's :insert() method.
	This is useful to change "altitude" of entities, ie their drawing order.

c. Creation of simple game entities
-----------------------------------

ENTITY x y a
	Create a "generic entity" (debug purpose) at (x,y) with angle a.

LABEL x y a text text2 [id]
	Create a label with text text at (x,y) with angle a. If id, attach it to entity id (x,y,a are then relative to id's).
	text2 can be reduced to a simple space (escaped) to signify an empty string.

IMAGE x y a N sprite1 [sprite2 ...] [r g b]
	Create a N-Image at (x,y,a) with strings sprite* as sprites, and optionnal color (r,g,b) as modulating color.
	
COUNTER x y [value]
	Create a counter at (x,y,a) with value value (which defaults to 0).

DIE x y [faces]
	Create a die at (x,y,a) with faces faces (which defaults to 6). The initial face is random.

CLOCK x y
	Create a clock at (x,y,a), initialised at 0 and in paused state.

d. Special commands to simple entities
--------------------------------------

SETLABEL id [text]
	Sets the current (top) text of the label id to text. If text is omitted, erase it instead (do not delete label).

ATTACH id [target]
	Attachs the label id to the entity target. If target is omitted, label is detached.

SETCOLOR id r g b
	Change the modulating color of N-Image id to (r,g,b).

SETCOUNTER id [value]
	Set the counter id to value, or 0 if ommited.

ROLL id [newrange]
	Roll die id. If a number newrange is given, sets the range of the die first.

RESETCLOCK id
TOGGLECLOCK id
	Reset or toggle (pause/unpause) clock id.

